      "type": "answer",
      "content": "ANSWER For this situation a Punnett square like that in Figure 2.2 would have allele frequencies $ p $ and $ q $ across the top, since these are the frequencies of $ A $ and $ a $ in female gametes; and it would have $ p' $ and $ q' $ along the side, since these are the frequencies of $ A $ and $ a $ in male gametes. After one generation of random mating, therefore, the genotype frequencies are $ pp' (AA) $, $ pq' + qp' (Aa) $, and $ qq' (aa) $. These frequencies apply equally to female and male offspring, since both parents contribute equally to the inheritance of an autosomal gene. The allele frequencies in both sexes in the offspring generation are therefore $ p^* = pp' + (pq' + qp') / 2 = (2pp' + pq' + qp') / 2 = [p(p' + q') + p' (p + q)] / 2 = (p + p') / 2 $ for $ A $ and likewise $ q^* = (q + q') / 2 $ for $ a $. (Note that these values are the averages of the allele frequencies in females and males of the previous generation.) Therefore, one generation of random mating is sufficient to equalize the allele frequencies in the sexes, and in subsequent generations the genotype frequencies will be given by the HWE with $ p = p^* $ and $ q = q^* $.",
